The '''political parties in [[Burawa]]''' operate within the constitutional framework of a {{W|socialism|socialist}} {{W|semi-presidential republic}}. The [[Constitution of Burawa]] requires that all parties uphold the state's obligations to guarantee the rights to {{W|healthcare}}, [[Housing in Burawa|housing]], food, happiness, equality, and privacy, and prohibits parties that do not adhere to these principles from participating in elections. Within this framework, thirteen parties currently hold seats in the [[Parliament of Burawa]], spanning a broad ideological spectrum from {{W|anarcho-syndicalism}} to {{W|social conservatism}}.

== Party system ==

=== Constitutional requirements ===

All political parties in Burawa must adhere to the socialist principles enshrined in the [[Constitution of Burawa|Constitution]]. Parties are required to accept the state's obligation to provide healthcare, housing, food, happiness, equality, and privacy to all citizens without discrimination on the basis of colour, class, or creed, and to accept the principle that all citizens contribute to the collective systems that sustain these rights. Challenges to a party's constitutional eligibility may be initiated by any citizen through the [[Local courts of Burawa|local courts]] and adjudicated through the [[Appellate courts of Burawa|appellate system]] to the [[High Court of Burawa]].

=== Formation and registration ===

A new political party is registered by filing paperwork with a [[Administrative divisions of Burawa|local administration]]. Constitutional eligibility is not reviewed at the time of registration; a party's adherence to the constitutional requirements is assessed only if a challenge is brought before the courts. Parties maintain formal internal structures, including conventions and internal elections for leadership.

=== Funding ===

Party funding in Burawa operates through three channels:

* '''State funding''' proportional to the number of parliamentary seats held.
* '''Party stipends''' available to parties that collect a qualifying number of citizen signatures, regardless of whether the party holds seats.
* '''Personal donations''', capped at 100 [[Hanti]] per person per year.

=== Media ===

Partisan media — newspapers, television channels, or other public media outlets operated by or on behalf of political parties — was prohibited as part of the post-[[1962 Crisis]] reforms. Parties are permitted to produce internal communications for their membership, including newsletters and digital media distributed through local party offices, but may not operate public-facing media organisations. Local party offices and youth wings serve as the primary venues for political engagement and recruitment.

=== Trade unions ===

{{W|Trade union|Trade unions}} in Burawa are constitutionally {{W|nonpartisanship|nonpartisan}} and do not formally affiliate with political parties. While individual union members may hold party memberships and unions may express positions on legislation, the institutional separation between the labour movement and the party system is maintained.

=== Campaigning ===

With partisan media prohibited, election campaigns in Burawa are conducted through door-to-door canvassing, public rallies, and televised or live-streamed debates. Leadership elections attract the most public attention, with nationally broadcast debates between candidates. Senate and Lower House elections are more localised; while debates are held at the commune level, most voters make their decisions on the basis of national-level party positioning, news coverage, and the prominence of the party's leadership rather than the individual qualities of local candidates. Campaign seasons are relatively short.

=== Axes of political disagreement ===

The principal axes of ideological variation among Burawi parties include:

* '''Economic liberalisation''': The degree to which non-essential industries should be opened to {{W|private sector|private enterprise}}, ranging from the [[Industrial Party of Burawa|Industrial Party]]'s advocacy for broad liberalisation to the [[Christian Socialist Union (Burawa)|Christian Socialist Union]]'s and [[Green Party of Burawa|Green Party]]'s opposition.
* '''Foreign policy''': Positions range from active promotion of socialism abroad ([[Party for Expansion of Socialism]], [[Forward Party of Burawa|Forward Party]]) to {{W|isolationism}} ([[Sago Revolutionary Party]]) to globalism without ideological export ([[Industrial Party of Burawa|Industrial Party]]).
* '''Social policy''': The progressive–conservative divide encompasses [[LGBT rights in Burawa|LGBT rights]], {{W|birth rate|pro-natalist}} policy, and the extent of social services coverage. Medical transitions, for example, are not currently covered under universal healthcare — a point of contention between progressive and conservative parties.
* '''Immigration''': Burawa operates a permissive but demanding immigration system in which foreign workers must maintain continuous employment for ten years to obtain citizenship, without family reunification. Positions range from the [[Party for Social Cohesion]]'s calls to end immigration entirely to the [[Forward Party of Burawa|Forward Party]]'s reforms expanding worker protections.
* '''Energy and environment''': Burawa historically transitioned away from fossil fuel dependency to avoid {{W|Dutch disease}}, but retains significant energy exports. Debate centres on the pace of transition to {{W|renewable energy}} for domestic consumption and whether fossil fuel extraction should be ended entirely.
* '''Metropolitan–rural divide''': The balance of investment, representation, and policy attention between the [[Administrative divisions of Burawa|eight metropolitan administrations]] (home to a majority of the population) and the rural interior.

== Coalition dynamics ==

As of 2026, three principal blocs operate in the [[Lower House of Burawa|Lower House]]:

The '''Socialist-led coalition''' comprises the [[Socialist Party of Burawa]], the [[Northern Socialist Party]], the [[Industrial Party of Burawa|Industrial Party]], and the [[Green Party of Burawa|Green Party]]. This bloc holds a narrow majority and forms the governing coalition under Prime Minister [[Iacob Naq]].

A separate '''progressive coalition''' has formed around the [[Reformed Christian Socialist Party (Burawa)|Reformed Christian Socialist Party]], the [[Communist Party of Burawa|Communist Party]], and the [[Party for Equitable Progress]]. The [[Forward Progressive Party]], which split from the Forward Party in March 2026, has aligned with this coalition and endorsed [[Muna Xasan]] in the [[2026 Burawi snap leadership election|snap leadership election]]. This coalition seeks to reconstitute the pre-2015 progressive bloc as a governing alternative, and operates independently of the Socialist-led coalition.

The [[National Party of Burawa|National Party]] and the [[Christian Socialist Union (Burawa)|Christian Socialist Union]] maintain an informal conservative coalition, competing for overlapping rural and religiously motivated voters while cooperating on legislation concerning agrarian policy and social conservatism.

The [[Forward Party of Burawa|Forward Party]], now led by [[Marik Sahamin]] following the March 2026 split that created the Forward Progressive Party, holds 51 Senate seats and 40 Lower House seats. The split significantly reduced the party's parliamentary strength, leaving it as a smaller opposition force rather than the Senate-dominant bloc it had been prior to the schism. The [[Forward Progressive Party]] now holds the larger share of the former Forward Party's representation, commanding 77 Senate seats and 60 Lower House seats.

== Major parties ==

=== Socialist Party of Burawa ===

{{main|Socialist Party of Burawa}}

