The '''[[Socialist Party of Burawa]]''' is the largest party in the [[Lower House of Burawa|Lower House]] and the dominant force in Burawi politics since the [[1962 Crisis]]. The party was founded by [[Kunciil Hanad]], a senior member of the original [[Northern and Central Socialist Party]] who broke with the [[Kadarist Party]] over its authoritarian direction. Hanad, who favoured a more liberal approach to governance, served first as [[Prime Minister of Burawa|Prime Minister]] before becoming [[Leader of the Burawi Republic]]. Under his leadership, the party orchestrated the comprehensive reforms of the 1960s that reshaped Burawa's political and economic system.

The Socialist Party held the Leadership continuously from 1990 to 2015, a period of sustained economic growth driven by the transition from a petroleum-dependent economy to one based on manufacturing and services. The party's long incumbency came under acute pressure following the [[Great Recession (Adonia)|Great Recession]] of 2009, which reduced global export demand and significantly affected Burawa's manufacturing- and export-centred economy. In response, the Socialist government instituted an emergency reduction in public-sector wage growth — ordinarily pegged at approximately three per cent per year — freezing wages at zero per cent in 2009 and raising them only incrementally over the following years (0.25% in 2010, 0.5% in 2011, 0.65% in 2012, 0.55% in 2013, 1.5% in 2014, and 2.5% in 2015). By the time wage growth approached its pre-crisis trajectory, public frustration with nearly eight years of stagnant purchasing power had consolidated, and the party's base had eroded sufficiently to enable [[Marik Sahamin]]'s victory in the [[2015 Burawi leadership election]].

On social policy, the Socialists passed the [[Domestic Partnership Act of 1996 (Burawa)|Domestic Partnership Act]] in 1995 but declined to legislate [[same-sex marriage]], opting instead to incrementally improve the existing domestic partnership framework rather than risk internal rifts with socially conservative factions. The party is generally described as progressive on paper but slow in translating stated positions into legislation.

The party supports the current [[Immigration in Burawa|immigration system]], favours maintaining energy exports while transitioning domestic capacity to {{W|renewable energy}}, and maintains internationalist alliances without actively promoting socialism abroad. The Socialist Party and the [[Northern Socialist Party]] maintain a standing parliamentary coalition.

As of 2026, Prime Minister [[Iacob Naq]] has adopted an increasingly adversarial posture toward both the [[Forward Party of Burawa|Forward Party]] and the [[National Party of Burawa|National Party]], having regained seats in the 2025 Lower House election. The Socialist-led bloc — comprising the Socialist Party, the [[Northern Socialist Party]], the [[Industrial Party of Burawa|Industrial Party]], and the [[Green Party of Burawa|Green Party]] — holds a narrow majority in the Lower House.

=== Forward Party of Burawa ===

