The '''[[Forward Party of Burawa]]''' was founded in 2014 by [[Marik Sahamin]] as a breakaway from the [[Socialist Party of Burawa|Socialist Party]]'s progressive and reform wing. Sahamin won the [[2015 Burawi leadership election]] at the age of 38 — a triple election in which all three electoral cycles coincided — making him the youngest [[Leader of the Burawi Republic]] in the country's history. The [[Progressive Socialist Party of Burawa]], formerly the principal vehicle of the progressive coalition, formally merged with the Forward Party in 2020, consolidating much of the progressive electorate under a single organisation.

The Forward Party is distinguished from the Socialist Party by its willingness to pursue ambitious and controversial policy action. During its decade in power (2015–2025), the party enacted the [[Clean Air and Transportation Act of 2020]], which banned most domestic commercial flights in favour of [[high-speed rail]]; passed a resolution recognising [[same-sex marriage]] in 2016 — one of the party's first legislative acts; authorised the [[2022 Sabia operation]], a one-day military intervention to depose the president of [[Sabia]]; negotiated the [[Kwalib reparations]]; and reformed the [[Immigration in Burawa|immigration system]] in 2019, introducing a 90-day grace period for workers who lose employment — replacing the previous requirement to restart the ten-year citizenship path entirely.

The party's foreign policy is characterised by the concept of a '''Burawi sphere of influence''' — the active economic and political management of neighbouring Kaftian states and former colonies. This interventionist posture, exemplified by the Sabia operation, generated both domestic support and significant opposition.

In February 2026, [[Marik Sahamin]] was removed as party leader by a {{W|vote of no confidence}} from the party congress. '''[[Warsame Cilmi]]''' was elected as his successor. However, on 26 March 2026, Sahamin announced his candidacy for the [[2026 Burawi snap leadership election|snap leadership election]], triggering a major party split. Approximately 60 per cent of the party's parliamentary caucus defected to form the '''[[Forward Progressive Party]]''' under [[Mahad Allaale]], while Sahamin retained the support of approximately 40 per cent of the caucus and reclaimed the party leadership. The Forward Progressive Party subsequently endorsed [[Muna Xasan]] of the [[Party for Equitable Progress]] in the leadership election.

