The '''[[National Party of Burawa]]''' was formed on 11 March 1986 through the merger of the [[Christian Socialist Party of Burawa]] and the [[Tenant Farmers Party of Burawa]]. The Christian Socialist Party was the principal political vehicle of Burawa's [[Burawi Catholic Church|Catholic]] communities, while the Tenant Farmers Party represented rural families engaged in the Burawi system of {{W|tenant farming}}, in which land is allocated by the state for cultivation and the sale of produce into the collective system. The merger consolidated the conservative and agrarian wings of Burawi politics.

In the decade following its formation, the Tenant Farmers Union's leadership assumed increasing control of the party's direction, shifting its emphasis away from religious policy toward agrarian and economic issues. This reorientation alienated some of the party's religiously motivated members but broadened its appeal in the rural interior. In 1996, the National Party voted in favour of the [[Domestic Partnership Act of 1996 (Burawa)|Domestic Partnership Act]], a decision driven by the legislation's provisions for greater local control — which the party's core constituency valued more than the social implications of the law in urban areas where the party held few seats. The vote precipitated the departure of the party's extreme social conservative wing, which formed the [[Christian Socialist Union (Burawa)|Christian Socialist Union]] on 5 April 1995.

The party's base lies in the rural administrations and small cities of the interior, particularly the [[Dhulsarren]] (the agricultural grain-producing region of northern Burawa). The National Party won the [[2025 Burawi leadership election]] under [[Hancil Thea]] — the first time in the party's history it captured the Leadership — through the accumulation of second-preference votes under the newly adopted {{W|ranked-choice voting}} system.

The party's trajectory was shaped by the [[Burawi High Speed Rail Scandal]] of 2019, in which [[Cabdirashiid Fure]], a representative from the [[Galbeed]] River area, was found to have conspired with other northern rural representatives to secure billions of [[hanti]] in funding for a parallel {{W|high-speed rail}} line between [[Sago]] and [[Lanwaley]], justified by falsified population growth projections. The completed route recorded the lowest ridership of any high-speed rail line in the country. Fure and other politicians were arrested. The government's response extended beyond individual prosecutions: the entire [[Dhulsarren]] region was subjected to collective sanctions, including funding restrictions, reduced growth allowances, and heightened scrutiny of regional representatives. The measures were perceived within the Dhulsarren as disproportionate collective punishment. [[Hancil Thea]], who had entered the Senate only the year before and had no connection to the scandal, emerged as the principal political voice of the Dhulsarren during the crisis, consolidating support among the region's remaining representatives and positioning himself as a figure of integrity from a community that had been nationally stigmatised.

The National Party is '''pro-immigration''', supports free trade, and favours assertive diplomacy with a militaristic posture. On social policy, the party occupies a moderate conservative position: it voted against the 2016 [[same-sex marriage]] resolution but no longer campaigns against it, preferring to maintain the current status quo rather than pursue either expansion or rollback of social rights. The party supports the export of socialism abroad and is open to further economic liberalisation.

== Minor parties with significant representation ==

