The '''[[Christian Socialist Union (Burawa)|Christian Socialist Union]]''' (CSU) was formed on 5 April 1996, one day after the [[Lower House of Burawa|Lower House]] passed the [[Domestic Partnership Act of 1996 (Burawa)|Domestic Partnership Act]]. The party originated as a split from the [[National Party of Burawa]], whose vote in favour of the legislation provoked the departure of its extreme social conservative wing. The split was driven by two distinct constituencies: members who objected specifically to the domestic partnership law, and members who had grown frustrated with the National Party's drift away from religious policy and sought a return to an explicitly faith-based political organisation.

In its early years, the CSU functioned as a coalition of these two currents — the socially conservative faction and a smaller contingent of progressive Christians who had felt marginalised by the National Party's increasing hostility toward religious factions. Over time, the party's agenda was shaped predominantly by its social conservative wing, which pushed increasingly reactionary positions on social issues. This trajectory culminated in a crisis in 2003, when leaked comments by party leader [[Binyaam Garaad]] disparaged non-[[Burawi Catholic Church|Burawi Catholic]] members of the party as forgivers of homosexuality, in response to a {{W|Protestantism|Protestant}} Burawi church that had begun officiating same-sex marriages — a practice unconnected to the CSU or its platform. The remarks precipitated the departure of the party's progressive Christian faction, which formed the [[Reformed Christian Socialist Party (Burawa)|Reformed Christian Socialist Party]].

The contemporary CSU draws its membership primarily from communities aligned with the more conservative and traditionalist currents of the Burawi Catholic Church. The party advocates mainstream socialism combined with {{W|social conservatism}}, {{W|protectionism}}, agrarianism, and nationalism, and is opposed to economic liberalisation.

