The '''[[Reformed Christian Socialist Party (Burawa)|Reformed Christian Socialist Party]]''' (Reformed CSP) was formed in 2003 as a breakaway from the [[Christian Socialist Union (Burawa)|Christian Socialist Union]], precipitated by leaked comments from the CSU's leadership that were perceived as hostile toward non-[[Burawi Catholic Church|Burawi Catholic]] party members. The split was the culmination of years of resentment within the CSU between members aligned with the Burawi Catholic Church and those from {{W|Protestantism|Protestant}} and mainline {{W|Catholic}} traditions.

The Reformed CSP represents a coalition of Protestant and mainline Catholic Burawis who adhere to more progressive theological interpretations, broadly aligned with the mainline [[Volisania|Volisanian]] Catholic tradition. The party retains the CSU's opposition to economic liberalisation but rejects its social conservatism, occupying a position that is among the most left-wing in Burawa on both social and economic axes.

== Small parties ==

=== Party for Equitable Progress ===

The '''[[Party for Equitable Progress]]''', led by [[Hibo Guure]], is a special-issue party focused on {{W|feminism}}, social progressivism, and [[LGBT rights in Burawa|LGBT rights]]. The party draws its support from urban regions and cooperates closely with the [[Forward Party of Burawa|Forward Party]] on social legislation. It holds no Senate seats and 22 Lower House seats.

=== Northern Socialist Party ===

The '''[[Northern Socialist Party]]''', led by [[Tomas Tuure]], traces its origins to the '''[[Dakari-Lake Socialist Union]]''', a political party of the [[First Republic of Burawa]] representing the [[Dakari people|Dakari]] and [[Great Kaftian Lake|Lake]] communities of northern Burawa. During the First Republic, the party's scope extended across a much larger territory, including the regions that became the independent states of [[Dakare]], [[West Dakare]], and [[Sarfone]] following the [[Act of Self-Determination]] of 1934, which permitted certain regions to leave the republic.

The party aligned itself with the [[Northern and Central Socialist Party]] during the [[socialist revolution]] but never formally merged, maintaining its distinct identity through an internal alliance arrangement. When the [[Kadarist Party]] lost public support following the [[1962 Crisis]], the Northern Socialists retained their regional stronghold and realigned with the newly formed [[Socialist Party of Burawa]]. The party is, in this sense, a surviving remnant of the First Republic's political landscape.

The Northern Socialist Party's contemporary base is concentrated in '''[[Dimar]]''', the most ethnically diverse region of Burawa. Dimar is home to significant populations of [[Dakari people|Dakari]], [[Tukulor people|Tukulor]], [[Lebu people|Lebu]], and [[Sarafoni people|Sarafoni]] — peoples whose ethnic kin in neighbouring countries chose independence in 1934, but whose communities within Dimar remained part of the republic. The party focuses on the rights and interests of these communities and of northern Burawa more broadly, and maintains a standing parliamentary coalition with the [[Socialist Party of Burawa|Socialist Party]]. It holds 8 Senate seats and 20 Lower House seats.

=== Party for Expansion of Socialism ===

The '''[[Party for Expansion of Socialism]]''' (PES), led by [[Dalmar Gaas]], advocates for {{W|accelerationism|accelerationist}} and {{W|vanguardism|vanguardist}} policies, seeking a substantially expanded role for Burawa in the promotion of international socialism. The party draws support from academics and highly educated professionals. The PES openly claims the ideological legacy of [[Kadarism]] — sometimes described as '''neo-[[Kadarism]]''' — the internationalist programme of the original [[Kadarist Party]] — and has experienced gradual growth in recent elections, attributed in part to nostalgic sentiment for the era of Burawa's most assertive foreign policy. The party competes directly with the [[Communist Party of Burawa|Communist Party]] for the far-left electorate. It holds no Senate seats and 18 Lower House seats.

=== Green Party of Burawa ===

The '''[[Green Party of Burawa]]''', led by [[Fartuun Dhegey]], is an {{W|eco-socialism|eco-socialist}} party predating the [[Forward Party of Burawa|Forward Party]]'s entry into environmental politics. The party maintains a stronger foothold in northern Burawa, where its support is concentrated in communities affected by {{W|industrial pollution}} and {{W|mining}} operations — particularly in areas where residents do not themselves work in those industries. The Green Party is distinguished from the Forward Party by its more radical orientation: where the Forward Party pursues environmental objectives through state action, the Green Party incorporates {{W|anarchism|anarchist}} and {{W|communism|communist}} messaging, advocating for decentralised and community-led environmental governance. The party also campaigns on animal rights and feminist issues. It holds 4 Senate seats and 8 Lower House seats.

=== Sago Revolutionary Party ===

The '''[[Sago Revolutionary Party]]''' (SRP) is a regionalist party founded in the 2020s by [[Bile Sago]], a former member of the [[National Party of Burawa|National Party]] who left the party and adopted the region's name as his surname. The SRP originated in part as a {{W|vaccine hesitancy|vaccine-sceptic}} and conspiracy-oriented movement before evolving into a broader platform of regional grievance centred on the [[Dafar]] region of western Burawa. The party's representation is confined to the [[Dafar Sare Rural Administration]] and the [[Dafar Hoose Rural Administration]], where it holds all parliamentary seats. The party is frequently characterised as a {{W|cult of personality}}. Bile Sago currently serves as the senator for the [[Dafar Sare Rural Administration]]. The party holds 1 Senate seat and 6 Lower House seats.

=== Red Block ===

The '''[[Red Block (Burawa)|Red Block]]''' is an {{W|anarchism|anarchist}} protest party. Most anarchist organisations in Burawa reject {{W|electoralism}}, but the Red Block ran a single candidate, [[Hodan Koosaar]], in the 2025 election — contesting a competitive seat held by a [[Forward Party of Burawa|Forward Party]] representative in a rural commune in the mountainous interior. The party holds no Senate seats and 1 Lower House seat.

== Parties without parliamentary representation ==

=== Party for Social Cohesion ===

The '''[[Party for Social Cohesion]]''' is a fringe party that advocates for the complete cessation of the [[Immigration in Burawa|immigration]] process. The party held seats in previous parliamentary terms but lost all representation in the 2025 election. Its position is considered outside the mainstream of Burawi politics, where all major parties support the continuation of the immigration system in some form.

=== Union-Communist Party ===

The '''[[Union-Communist Party (Burawa)|Union-Communist Party]]''' is an alternative {{W|syndicalism|syndicalist}} party distinguished from the [[Communist Party of Burawa]] by its more socially conservative orientation. The party held seats in previous parliamentary terms but lost all representation in the 2025 election.

== Extra-parliamentary movements ==

Several anarchist organisations operate outside the electoral system in Burawa. These movements are concentrated in two settings: student organisations at universities, and rural communities in less economically productive areas — particularly in the north and in mountainous jungle regions distant from mining operations — where communal social hierarchies provide a foundation for anarchist organising. These movements reject electoralism and do not participate in the formal political process.

== Historical parties ==

=== Northern and Central Socialist Party ===

The '''[[Northern and Central Socialist Party]]''' (NCSP) was the party that led the [[socialist revolution]] of the 1930s and drafted the [[Constitution of Burawa|Constitution of 1932]]. Under [[Maana Kadar]], the NCSP governed the new republic and created the office of [[Leader of the Burawi Republic]]. Following Kadar's death in the 1940s, the party was reconstituted as the [[Kadarist Party]].

=== Kadarist Party ===

The '''[[Kadarist Party]]''', successor to the NCSP, pursued an internationalist policy of exporting socialism in coordination with [[Daras]] and [[Balisca]]. The party's neglect of domestic wages and housing, combined with diplomatic isolation from non-socialist neighbours, contributed to the [[1962 Crisis]]. Following a failed military {{W|coup d'état}} and an overwhelming electoral defeat, the Kadarist Party fragmented into several successor organisations that were eventually absorbed into the modern party system.

=== Dakari-Lake Socialist Union ===

The '''[[Dakari-Lake Socialist Union]]''' was a party of the [[First Republic of Burawa]] representing the [[Dakari people|Dakari]] and [[Great Kaftian Lake|Lake]] communities of northern Burawa. The party aligned with the [[Northern and Central Socialist Party]] during the revolution but maintained its organisational independence. It survives in the modern era as the [[Northern Socialist Party]].

=== Progressive Socialist Party of Burawa ===

The '''[[Progressive Socialist Party of Burawa]]''' was the principal party of the progressive wing of Burawi politics prior to 2015. Together with the [[Reformed Christian Socialist Party (Burawa)|Reformed Christian Socialist Party]], the [[Party for Equitable Progress]], the [[Green Party of Burawa|Green Party]], and the [[Red Block (Burawa)|Red Block]], the Progressive Socialists formed a progressive coalition that periodically alternated parliamentary control with the [[Socialist Party of Burawa|Socialist Party]]. The party merged with the [[Forward Party of Burawa]] in 2020.

=== Mazar Party ===

The '''[[Mazar Party]]''' was the political vehicle of [[Mazar]]'s merchant and industrial class. On 18 June 1933, following the [[socialist revolution]], the party was renamed the [[Industrial Party of Burawa]] and reconstituted as a socialist party in compliance with the new [[Constitution of Burawa|Constitution]].

=== Christian Socialist Party of Burawa ===

The '''[[Christian Socialist Party of Burawa]]''' was the principal political vehicle of Burawa's [[Burawi Catholic Church|Catholic]] communities prior to the formation of the [[National Party of Burawa|National Party]] on 11 March 1986. It merged with the [[Tenant Farmers Party of Burawa]] to form the National Party. The Christian Socialist Party's legacy persists in two successor organisations: the [[Christian Socialist Union (Burawa)|Christian Socialist Union]] (1996) and the [[Reformed Christian Socialist Party (Burawa)|Reformed Christian Socialist Party]] (2003), both of which trace their origins to religious factions that departed the National Party after its secular turn.

=== Tenant Farmers Party of Burawa ===

The '''[[Tenant Farmers Party of Burawa]]''' represented rural families engaged in Burawa's {{W|tenant farming}} system, in which land is allocated by the state for cultivation and the sale of produce into the collective system. The party merged with the [[Christian Socialist Party of Burawa]] to form the [[National Party of Burawa|National Party]] in the 1980s.

== Key political issues ==

=== Immigration ===

{{main|Immigration in Burawa}}

Burawa operates a permissive but demanding immigration system. Employers may hire foreign workers and sponsor {{W|work permit|work visas}}. To obtain [[Burawi citizenship]], a foreign worker must maintain continuous employment in Burawa for ten years. Workers may not bring family members during this period. If a worker's employment is terminated, the worker has 90 days to secure new employment — a provision introduced by the [[Forward Party of Burawa|Forward Party]]'s 2019 immigration reform. Prior to this reform, a worker who lost employment was required to leave the country and restart the ten-year path entirely.

The largest recent immigrant populations originate from [[Enfolo]], [[Desket]], and [[Lacashe]] — countries outside [[Southwestern Kaftia]] whose citizens seek economic opportunities not available domestically. Immigration from Burawa's immediate neighbours in Southwestern Kaftia is comparatively limited, as these countries maintain more stable economic conditions and manage cross-border migration through bilateral agreements. The majority of immigrant workers are employed in {{W|mining}} and {{W|heavy industry}}, sectors classified as essential under Burawi law and therefore operated by the state. These industries are regarded as undesirable or dangerous by most Burawi citizens, creating a structural dependence on foreign labour.

Immigration is a significant axis of political disagreement. The [[Party for Social Cohesion]], a fringe party, advocates for the complete cessation of immigration. The [[National Party of Burawa|National Party]] and the [[Socialist Party of Burawa|Socialist Party]] support the current system. The [[Forward Party of Burawa|Forward Party]] has pursued reforms to expand worker protections within the existing framework.

=== Energy and environment ===

Burawa historically transitioned away from dependence on {{W|petroleum}} and {{W|natural gas}} exports to avoid {{W|Dutch disease}}, diversifying into manufacturing and services. The country retains significant fossil fuel extraction capacity and continues to export energy, but debate centres on the pace of domestic transition to {{W|renewable energy}} and whether extraction should be ended entirely.

The [[Forward Party of Burawa|Forward Party]] enacted the [[Clean Air and Transportation Act of 2020]], which banned most domestic commercial flights in favour of [[high-speed rail]], as part of an {{W|eco-socialism|eco-socialist}} programme. The [[Green Party of Burawa|Green Party]] advocates for more radical environmental measures, including the complete cessation of fossil fuel extraction. The [[Socialist Party of Burawa|Socialist Party]] supports maintaining energy exports while transitioning domestic consumption to renewables.

=== Social policy ===

[[Same-sex marriage]] was recognised in Burawa by resolution in 2016, one of the first legislative acts of the [[Forward Party of Burawa|Forward Party]] government. The [[Socialist Party of Burawa|Socialist Party]], while supportive of same-sex marriage in principle, had declined to legislate it during its decades in power, preferring incremental improvements to the existing [[Domestic Partnership Act of 1996 (Burawa)|Domestic Partnership Act]] (enacted in 1995 with the support of the [[National Party of Burawa|National Party]]) over the risk of internal conflict with socially conservative factions. The National Party's vote in favour of the domestic partnership law — motivated by the legislation's provisions for local control — precipitated the departure of its social conservative wing and the formation of the [[Christian Socialist Union (Burawa)|Christian Socialist Union]].

[[LGBT rights in Burawa]] remain a point of political contention. While same-sex marriage and basic protections are recognised, medical transitions are not covered under universal healthcare — a position contested by the [[Party for Equitable Progress]] and the [[Forward Party of Burawa|Forward Party]], and opposed by the [[Christian Socialist Union (Burawa)|Christian Socialist Union]] and the [[National Party of Burawa|National Party]].

Burawa's {{W|total fertility rate}} has declined year-on-year but remains relatively high compared to most advanced economies. The country's original pro-natalist mechanism is structural: the [[Housing in Burawa|Burawi Housing System]] allocates housing space on the basis of household size, granting 25 m² for a single resident and an additional 28 m² per additional household member. Marriage and children therefore substantially increase a household's living space. However, this incentive has proven insufficient to maintain replacement-level fertility, as younger generations increasingly forgo marriage and child-rearing — a trend attributed to social and cultural shifts rather than economic constraints. Proposals under discussion include a nationalised {{W|childcare}} system and direct financial incentives, though the government has historically maintained a hands-off approach to reproductive policy.

=== Foreign policy and the Burawi sphere of influence ===

Burawa exercises significant economic and political influence over [[Southwestern Kaftia]] and parts of [[Abyala]] with historical ties to the [[Colonial History of Burawa|Burawi colonial period]]. This influence — sometimes described as a '''Burawi sphere of influence''' — is not a formal policy doctrine but a recognised tendency in Burawi foreign affairs, encompassing trade agreements, military alliances, development aid, and diplomatic pressure.

The [[Forward Party of Burawa|Forward Party]], during its decade in power (2015–2025), pursued this tendency most actively, framing it as the promotion of socialism abroad. The [[2022 Sabia operation]] — a one-day military intervention to depose the president of a neighbouring state — represented its most controversial expression. The [[National Party of Burawa|National Party]] under [[Hancil Thea]] has signalled continuity with the sphere-of-influence approach but with a different emphasis: reduced commitments to environmental standards imposed on partner states, increased energy exports to the region, and strengthened military alliances. The [[Industrial Party of Burawa|Industrial Party]] opposes the export of socialism abroad, while the [[Party for Expansion of Socialism]] advocates for a substantially more aggressive programme of international socialist promotion.

